section('## Abstract','## 1. Introduction', '''## Abstract

Individual biomedical records may be inaccessible or omit variables needed for a scientific analysis, while relevant summaries and associations are available in published studies. We investigate whether a scientific Agent can turn that partial evidence into useful complete virtual cohorts without individual-level construction records. The Agent interprets findings under their original study conditions, chooses a joint generating law, translates usable statistics into numerical constraints and records assumptions for unreported relationships. Numerical tools execute and inspect the construction. In a developmental study using CHNS and ENSANUT references, 20 Agent and four statistical constructions produced 72 complete tables; additional relational evidence reduced mean absolute discrepancy for five of six specified coefficients. Separate experiments demonstrated supplied-summary reconstruction and longitudinal analysis from published model artifacts. A unified open-retrieval workflow then produced twelve 3,000-person tables from four fresh Agent constructions across both population requests. All prespecified coefficient directions agreed with the existing sample references, while effect magnitudes, rank dependence and residual variability remained imperfect. An earlier analytical sensitivity identified an unreported dependence capable of reversing a conditional coefficient despite unchanged marginal relationships. These results support repeated evidence-to-cohort construction and analysis-specific usefulness, while exposing the joint-model and transport assumptions that limit scientific interpretation.''')
replace('The paper makes three contributions. **First**, it implements an Agent workflow in which the scientific author selects and numerically uses evidence, chooses the generating law and makes a documented retain-or-revise decision. **Second**, it makes evidence-to-model decisions inspectable: we demonstrate the translation of separate adjusted projections into joint coefficients and the identification of a missing dependence that controls a requested conditional effect. **Third**, it evaluates the resulting data through ordinary scientific analyses under external-evidence, supplied-summary and published-model information settings, together with a completed open-search construction.',
'''The paper makes three contributions. **First**, it implements and repeatedly exercises an evidence-to-cohort workflow: from a biomedical request, the Agent selects studies and their numerical roles, authors a joint law and delivers analysable data without individual construction records. **Second**, it makes scientific modeling decisions inspectable, including reconciliation of separate adjusted projections and explicit assumptions for missing dependence; a worked sensitivity shows how such a dependence changes a conditional conclusion. **Third**, it evaluates the data through ordinary scientific analyses under complementary information conditions: a larger external-evidence contrast, supplied-summary reconstruction, a published longitudinal model and repeated open-retrieval constructions. The contribution is a qualified, reusable biomedical data-generation procedure, rather than a new estimator or a claim that every inferred relationship is identified.''')
