section('### 4.5. Q4:', '## 5. Discussion', '''### 4.5. Q4: repeated open retrieval produces useful complete cohorts

The unified workflow allocated two fresh scientific authors to each of the existing CHNS and ENSANUT requests. Each author received the intended analyses, population/measurement definitions, task interface and retrieval budget, but no target coefficients, desired signs, individual records or prior construction. CHNS- and ENSANUT-derived studies were excluded as generating evidence. Across 24 query credits, the authors selected sixteen paper uses representing twelve distinct originals. Independent review checked the original statistical definitions, numerical derivations, source applicability and required finite moments before execution. Sources could overlap across authors; four constructions do not imply four independent bodies of clinical evidence.

The Agents chose materially different laws. C01 used an age-conditioned multivariate-lognormal mixture informed by Chinese population summaries [7,16] and contextual age/body-size findings [10,17]. C02 combined copula predictors with an affine HbA1c conditional mean using Pinggu summaries and Singapore coefficients [18,19], with other populations as applicability context [14,20]. E01 used bounded beta predictors and a conditional gamma response [10,17,21,22]. E02 combined lognormal BMI, conditional truncated-normal glucose and a positive conditional-mean lognormal response [10,21,23,24]. Their unreported dependencies, population transfers and residual choices remained declared assumptions. The shared controller did not prescribe these families or insert target coefficients.

Each author completed one 3,000-row trial, inspected generated-only diagnostics and source feedback, then selected its final model before three fixed draws. All four retained the initial law. The sixteen physical executions comprised four trials and twelve unique final tables, or 36,000 final virtual rows. Feedback contained 29 qualified approximate comparisons and 39 unavailable checks across the four trials, not source-population replays or 29 passed constraints. After all sessions closed, the evaluator reused the fixed sample references. A separate numerical implementation reproduced all 366 final coefficient/diagnostic values; no clinical data were downloaded or refitted.

All 36 primary seed estimates had the same positive directions as their corresponding references (Figure 5). These are repeated measurements of six analysis definitions across four laws, not 36 independent confirmations. Magnitude recovery varied by analysis. CHNS C01/C02 means were 0.03992/0.05338 for BMI against 0.05495, 0.37754/0.49241 for glucose against 0.40209, and 0.00311/0.00563 for age against 0.00811. Units are respectively HbA1c percentage points per kg/m², mmol/L and year. C02 was closer for BMI and age, while C01 was closer for glucose; no construction dominated all analyses.

![Repeated open-retrieval scientific analyses](paper_figures_v13_2026-11-04/fig5-open-retrieval.svg)

**Figure 5. Repeated open-evidence construction across two population requests.** All six prespecified primary analyses are shown in native units. C01/C02 and E01/E02 are separately authored generators for CHNS and ENSANUT requests. Colored circles show all three final seed estimates per author, diamonds their means and horizontal segments their min–max ranges; these ranges are not confidence intervals. A separate coral reference row displays the existing sample estimate and either a CHNS community-cluster CR1-t 95% interval or an ENSANUT empirical cluster-resampling stability band. Neither interval is an acceptance margin. All twelve tables and 36 primary estimates are retained. Reference values were withheld from these authors but were previously exposed during project development. The figure tests repeated construction and analysis-specific agreement, not new clinical validation or revision gain; all four initial laws were retained.

For ENSANUT, E01/E02 means were 0.10308/0.09772 for adjusted age against 0.07513 pp/decade, 0.09165/0.07140 for age/sex-adjusted BMI against 0.08364 pp per 5 kg/m², and 0.07509/0.04733 after additionally adjusting for glucose against 0.05664. Every draw retained the reduction of the BMI coefficient after glucose adjustment, an association pattern rather than causal mediation. Both authors built an age coefficient near 0.10 pp/decade and shared some sources; their agreement does not establish independent evidence convergence.

The complete diagnostics reveal remaining limits. CHNS C01 glucose–HbA1c Pearson correlation was approximately 0.666 versus 0.661, but Spearman correlation was 0.635 versus 0.361. ENSANUT residual HbA1c RMS was approximately 0.295/0.324 versus 0.488, and total SD was 0.362/0.406 versus 0.527. Correct directions and a close Pearson coefficient therefore coexisted with rank and dispersion mismatches. The [complete replication report](../work/core_workflow_replication_2026-11-04/RESULTS.md), every diagnostic and Supplementary Table S1 retain the full results. A scalar-type interface defect in the original evaluator was corrected additively on unchanged frozen tables, with its failed output and independent audit preserved; no scientific model was adjusted after comparison.

### 4.6. A worked evidence gap explains analysis-specific sensitivity

The preceding open-search example acquired five seriously reviewed studies [9,10,12,13,14] without bibliographic seeds or desired signs. A fresh constructor selected a sex-stratified Gaussian copula with positive biomarker margins and generated one trial plus three complete 3,000-person tables. It retained the model after eleven approximate and 23 unavailable source comparisons per execution. Secondary BMI coefficients were 0.04217–0.05176 versus 0.05495 in the reused CHNS reference, secondary age was 0.01613–0.01771 versus 0.01530, and primary age was 0.00842–0.01084 versus 0.00811. Primary BMI/glucose were attenuated, and the female point direction and distributional tails remained imperfect. Supplementary Figure S1 retains all seven nonintercept effects and their uncertainty labels.

Before generating data or comparing targets, the source review identified an unreported BMI–glucose dependence. Two valid latent correlations, 0.10 and 0.40, implied conditional BMI coefficients of +0.03422 and −0.01095 while retaining the same margins, other bivariate laws and secondary projection. The delivered model used the declared assumption 0.25. This is an analytical sensitivity, not additional generated cohorts or a causal intervention. It demonstrates why an explicit data-generating law can identify which missing relationship matters to an intended use.

A separate analysis contextualized the same three tables with 100 community-disjoint resplits of the existing CHNS sample, drawing 3,000 people per side and retaining all 36 metrics. Secondary BMI and age discrepancies were on scales comparable with within-sample variation, while primary glucose/female and several joint/tail discrepancies were larger. The [complete table](../work/real_real_calibration_2026-11-04/COMPLETE_METRIC_TABLE.md) preserves the nonexchangeable real-real and generated comparisons. These are descriptive interpretation aids, not a new population or retrospectively selected equivalence cutoff.''')
section('## 5. Discussion', '## Supporting results, code and reproducibility', '''## 5. Discussion

The results support a practical role for a scientific Agent: translate dispersed, study-qualified biomedical evidence into a complete data product that ordinary analysis software can use. The larger information contrast, supplied-summary reconstruction and longitudinal example show complementary kinds of analytical usefulness. The repeated open-retrieval experiment adds evidence that the procedure can complete new constructions with different retrieved studies and generating families under a shared policy. It extends workflow replication; it does not add new clinical reference populations.

The methodological contribution lies in the connection between a scientific request, heterogeneous evidence and an executable joint law. Statistical transformations and simulation are established tools [3–6]; the Agent selects which findings can constrain that law, derives their numerical roles and exposes the assumptions needed to complete it. The source-to-model trace and dependence sensitivity make those consequential decisions inspectable. G-Sim and LLMSynthor also give LLMs important construction and feedback roles, so Agent participation alone is not a priority claim. Here the applied biomedical problem, low individual-data requirements, explicit study conditions and analysis-level evaluation define the contribution.

Useful virtual data need not duplicate a particular real sample. Nevertheless, usefulness depends on the requested analysis. Additional relations improved five main-study discrepancies while CHNS BMI worsened. Open-retrieval authors preserved directions but showed systematic age displacement and compressed ENSANUT residual variability. Increasing the number of generated rows would reduce conditional Monte Carlo variation without identifying missing dependence or repairing population transfer. These data may support exploratory relationship analysis under documented assumptions; the present dispersion results do not validate downstream power calculations or interval coverage.

Three boundaries determine the interpretation. First, CHNS and ENSANUT are exposed developmental references with partly shared source evidence. Summary-assisted R and the HRS example intentionally receive relevant numerical information. Fresh author contexts and delayed reference reads preserve construction boundaries but do not restore untouched external validation. Second, three seeds and conditional model intervals do not quantify source-estimation, extraction, structural or transport uncertainty. The real-real context describes one selected sample, not between-population variation. Third, the demonstrated workflow is host-dispatched and reviewed; arbitrary-request autonomy, broad biochemical coverage and total preparation-time savings have not been measured. Different author laws demonstrate construction flexibility, not a calibrated uncertainty ensemble.

Feedback is operational, but improvement from revision remains unestablished. Seven main-study authors reviewed exploratory outputs and retained their laws; thirteen finalized without exploration. All six L/R authors, the preceding open-search constructor, the two earlier live authors and all four new workflow authors also retained their models after available review. One earlier live author had repaired a coordinator-detected divergent-moment defect before freeze; that repair is distinct from a demonstrated feedback-driven gain in analytical quality. These records show inspection and valid retention. They do not justify an iterative-improvement claim or an automatic requirement to revise a coherent model. Historical enrichment, direct-row construction, failed transport criteria, tail defects and revision trade-offs remain in the [historical supplement](SUPPLEMENT_HISTORICAL_RESULTS_v05.md); missing-cell completion is unvalidated.

The current evidence supports a scoped applied-method paper without requiring superiority over every purpose-built generator. A stronger transport claim would require a prospectively frozen, previously unexposed reference evaluation. The most focused optional mechanism question is sensitivity to unsupported conditional-dispersion and dependence assumptions while holding retrieved evidence fixed, preserving every analysis rather than selecting a target-fitted winner. Neither extension is needed to describe the completed capability honestly: Agents can construct useful virtual biomedical data from published evidence, and explicit joint models show where the scientific support ends.''')
